<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Graufesenque</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Graufesenque is an archaeological site near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millau">Millau</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the remains of the Roman pottery centre of Condatomagus. In the 1st century CE its workshops produced vast quantities of red-gloss terra sigillata pottery, exported across the Roman world from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Kiln records listing the potters' firings survive on fragments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-graufesenque.jpg" alt="La Graufesenque" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nfhudson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graugresenque_-_sigillata.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
